WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 6 Leader Video
Unit 2, Lesson 2: Monotheism

1
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.100
Well, my favorite passages in scripture is Exodus 6.

2
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.980
Why Exodus 6?

3
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:17.180
Well, it's not because of Moses, believe it or not.

4
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.980
And it's not because that Moses is

5
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.700
on the brink of the Israelites leaving Egypt

6
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.340
and this really cool escape plan

7
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.820
that Moses is going to lead the nation of Israel on.

8
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.500
No, believe it or not, here's why.

9
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.460
Because we discover something about God

10
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.500
that we did not previously know

11
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.500
from the very beginning of scripture

12
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.420
all the way up to now.

13
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.020
In verse one of chapter six,

14
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.980
the Lord said to Moses,

15
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.700
now you will see what I will do to Pharaoh.

16
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.020
Because of my mighty hand, he will let them go.

17
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.380
Because of my mighty hand,

18
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.860
he will drive them out of his country.

19
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.220
God also said to Moses,

20
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.100
I am the Lord.

21
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.740
I appear to Abraham, to Isaac,

22
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.460
and to Jacob as God Almighty.

23
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.820
But by my name, the Lord,

24
00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.340
I did not make myself known to them.

25
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.020
I also established my covenant with them

26
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.700
to give them the land of Canaan

27
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.220
where they lived as aliens.

28
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.380
Moreover, I have heard the groaning of the Israelites

29
00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.420
whom the Egyptians are enslaving

30
00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.740
and I have remembered my covenant.

31
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.660
I love this passage

32
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.500
because this is where we start to see God

33
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.500
in a different light.

34
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.460
More than just this idea of God Almighty.

35
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.060
So Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob

36
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.540
sort of saw God as God Almighty.

37
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.300
And all that means is

38
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.420
the God that's better than every other God,

39
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.900
the Creator God,

40
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.260
just this sort of person up here.

41
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.580
Now there's some relational stuff going on

42
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.020
with those three,

43
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.260
but not in the same way that we see it with Moses.

44
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.380
He makes the covenant,

45
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.260
but here he remembers the covenant

46
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.420
and the covenant's becoming more of a reality

47
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.940
than it was before.

48
00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.100
This is where we start to see

49
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.580
the personal nature of God.

50
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.340
And that is incredibly important

51
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.020
when we start talking about monotheism.

52
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.780
Because we're not just talking about

53
00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.260
the fact that a singular God exists.

54
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.180
We're talking about something slightly more than that.

55
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.860
Now, we're talking about naturalism.

56
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.460
We talked about the dominant view of the West.

57
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.620
We talked about pantheism,

58
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.660
the dominant view of the East.

59
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.380
We talked about the conversions of the two

60
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.060
and how they don't work on the buffet.

61
00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.460
We talked about all of those things.

62
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.300
Monotheism really is the view

63
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:42.420
that God has made himself known.

64
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.660
That God has made himself known to us

65
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.260
in a very personal and very real way.

66
00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.620
But, so before we need to make a case

67
00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.900
for Christianity to be true,

68
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.420
before we go any further, right?

69
00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.500
We've talked about what truth is

70
00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.820
and logic and faith and apologetics.

71
00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:58.980
We've talked about worldviews

72
00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.780
and we've talked about naturalism.

73
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.140
We've talked about pantheism.

74
00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.820
Before we go any further,

75
00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.500
before we talk about the resurrection

76
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:06.980
and before we talk about scripture

77
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.340
and before we talk about how God has made himself known,

78
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.980
we need to be able to show

79
00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.980
that there is a single, personal, all-powerful creator.

80
00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.740
So I wanna make something really clear here.

81
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:24.540
We're talking about monotheism as a worldview.

82
00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.180
So within that worldview,

83
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.660
you have other religions as well.

84
00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:30.620
You have Judaism, you have Christianity,

85
00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.460
but you also have Islam.

86
00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.300
Now, when we're talking about the buffet

87
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.300
of a bunch of worldviews,

88
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.940
when we're talking about monotheism,

89
00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.180
we're talking about, listen,

90
00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.700
we're gonna put the buffet aside

91
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.500
and we're gonna order off a menu

92
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.500
and get the actual dish

93
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:48.340
that we know is the best possible dish

94
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.420
that satisfies us the most,

95
00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.140
gives us the proper nutrients.

96
00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.220
In other words,

97
00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.820
the worldview that is most consistent,

98
00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:58.740
the worldview that is most coherent,

99
00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:03.740
and the worldview that speaks to the reality the best.

100
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.900
Now, we're gonna talk about specifically next week

101
00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.900
and moving forward of why Christianity

102
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.900
within the framework of monotheism is true.

103
00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.340
And the rest of them will go away.

104
00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.900
But for this right now,

105
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.420
we're just talking about monotheism in general.

106
00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.180
And we're talking about the idea of a loving, personal God.

107
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.620
Now, when we talked about pantheism,

108
00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.500
we talked about the idea of that there's,

109
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.460
that there needs to be some kind of distinction, right?

110
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:33.900
So, we're gonna take that distinction

111
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:35.380
and take it one step further

112
00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.420
and talk specifically about the idea

113
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.260
of a personal, caring God.

114
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.900
But I could take that even one step further.

115
00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.620
If you go back to Exodus six,

116
00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.440
you look at what God says.

117
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.980
It says, I am the Lord,

118
00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:53.700
I appeared to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob as God Almighty.

119
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.260
I appeared to them in one way.

120
00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.980
But by myself, by my name, the Lord,

121
00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:05.140
this is again going back to the idea of Yahweh,

122
00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:08.740
I am who I am, the name of God,

123
00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:13.260
the utter holiness of God, the other thanness of God.

124
00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:17.500
God takes who he is a step further in front of Moses

125
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.140
and says, I am more than just God Almighty

126
00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:21.820
over everything else.

127
00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:24.860
I am who I am, Yahweh.

128
00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.940
And they kind of describe what that looks like

129
00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.500
and we can add into our focus

130
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.800
the idea of a God who redeems.

131
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.820
A God who is not just out there creating things

132
00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.500
and leaving it alone.

133
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.460
We're talking about a God

134
00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.100
who is actively engaged in history,

135
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.420
redeeming, pursuing his own creation,

136
00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.140
that is made in his image.

137
00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:51.780
That he is chasing after us,

138
00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.420
even after we screwed it up.

139
00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.500
He is chasing us down, drawing us back to him

140
00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:02.000
because of his love for us.

141
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.460
That's pretty profound.

142
00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.580
That sets us up for what Jesus does.

143
00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.100
That sets us up for belief in the Holy Spirit.

144
00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.220
That sets us up for the belief in the Trinity

145
00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:14.820
that gives us a better understanding of who God is

146
00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.340
as we move into really what this idea

147
00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.560
of monotheism actually looks like.

148
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.420
So we've had you take your students,

149
00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.020
especially in small groups

150
00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.740
and working through the Bible study in large group

151
00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.300
sort of really get a feel for

152
00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:31.100
how naturalism and how pantheism

153
00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.260
sort of has infiltrated and influenced our current culture.

154
00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:38.440
I want you to sort of back out of that for a second

155
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.700
and sort of return to tables.

156
00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.260
I want you to explore with your students now

157
00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:47.060
how looking at the world through the eyes

158
00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.380
of a monotheistic worldview,

159
00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.460
how that could actually influence the culture.

160
00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:54.860
Because ultimately as Christians,

161
00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:57.220
we are called to get into our culture

162
00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.500
and impact our culture for the gospel.

163
00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.020
So what does that look like?

164
00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.600
So how then does your worldview change?

165
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:04.900
So even for your students

166
00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:06.860
who maybe don't see the world

167
00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:09.500
as a monotheistic through a monotheistic worldview

168
00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.940
just yet,

169
00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:12.980
encourage them to take a step back

170
00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.220
and ask them what would it look like

171
00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.820
if I viewed the world that way?

172
00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:23.820
What would it look like if I saw God as one God,

173
00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.220
more than one God as a personal God,

174
00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.300
more than just a personal God as one who cares

175
00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:31.980
and is actually pursuing me individually?

176
00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.840
How would that change your students' lives?

177
00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.940
How would that change their thoughts on morality?

178
00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:38.800
How would that change their thoughts on justice?

179
00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.820
How would that change their thoughts on love

180
00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.860
on their relationships with their friends and their family?

181
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.260
How would it even change how they function at school?

182
00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.620
How would their entire lives change

183
00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.180
if they thought that way, right?

184
00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:55.500
So have them explore what that looks like

185
00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:58.260
and encourage them to sort of maybe try it out

186
00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:00.180
and see what happens.

187
00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.460
And then as we continue to move forward,

188
00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.820
we'll be building the case

189
00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:07.900
that within the monotheistic framework,

190
00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:11.660
Christianity represents absolute truth.

